      "note": "concerned that Guix-only linker flags would hide linker issues from developers; resolved by moving flags to general build"
    },
    {
      "login": "hebasto",
      "stance": "objection",
      "note": "Concept ACK on ICF, but objected that -faddrsig is required for Darwin and noted safe shadows safe_thunks in probe order"
    },
    {
      "login": "151henry151",
      "stance": "objection",
      "note": "not an ACK as-is; proved address-significant function folding on Darwin LLD 19 and analyzed probe order"
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "c:4499825348",
      "kind": "maintenance",
      "harm": "Enabling ICF only for Guix builds hides potential linker issues from developers building locally.",
      "quote": "I worry that if any linker tricks like this cause any trouble, we'd only see it in guix binaries.",
      "blocking": false,
      "status": "resolved",
      "author_replies": [
        "c:4875779934"
      ],
      "fix_pushed": true,
      "settled_by_id": "c:4875779934",
      "settled_by_quote": "I've pulled this back out of REDUCE_EXPORTS, into the general flags."
    },
    {
      "id": "c:4461546707",
      "kind": "correctness",
      "harm": "Without -faddrsig, --icf=safe on Mach-O targets behaves like --icf=all, breaking function pointer uniqueness and folding address-significant functions.",
      "quote": "My LLM friend thinks that `--icf=safe` is not actually safe, because without an address-significance table (`-faddrsig`) it's equivalent to `--icf=all`.",
      "blocking": true,
      "status": "open",
      "author_replies": [
        "c:4461591584",
        "c:5238854292"
      ],
      "fix_pushed": false,
      "settled_by_id": "",
      "settled_by_quote": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "rc:3652466874",
      "kind": "approach",
      "harm": "The linker flag probe order appends --icf=safe after --icf=safe_thunks, which causes safe to override safe_thunks on toolchains supporting both.",
      "quote": "This prioritizes `--icf=safe` over `--icf=safe_thunks` on the linker command line. However, shouldn't it be the other way around, since `safe_thunks` does not rely on the address-significance table?",
      "blocking": false,
      "status": "open",
      "author_replies": [],
      "fix_pushed": false,
      "settled_by_id": "",
      "settled_by_quote": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "c:5136916557",
      "kind": "correctness",
      "harm": "On the pinned Guix toolchain, LLD 19 Mach-O folds address-taken functions unless -faddrsig is passed across core and depends, corrupting dispatch tables and function identity.",
      "quote": "Not an ACK in its current shape — on the pinned Guix toolchain, `--icf=safe` behaves like `--icf=all` for the Darwin release binaries. Adding `-faddrsig` fixes that and keeps most of the size win",
      "blocking": true,
      "status": "open",
      "author_replies": [],
      "fix_pushed": false,
      "settled_by_id": "",
      "settled_by_quote": ""
    }
  ],
  "support": [
    {
      "id": "c:4446990079",
      "verdict": "ACK",
      "reason": "nice win in binary sizes, with Guix size benchmarks showing ~5% savings across Darwin binaries",
      "substantive": true
    },
    {
      "id": "r:4781720529",
      "verdict": "Concept ACK",
      "reason": "on using the \"Identical Code Folding\" linker feature where possible.",
      "substantive": true
    }
  ],
  "notes": "First read of the PR. Sjors and 151henry151 demonstrate that --icf=safe without -faddrsig folds address-significant functions on Darwin Mach-O, leaving this change blocked until -faddrsig is incorporated and probe ordering is resolved."
}
